=========================================================
PART A: Variables and operators
=========================================================

A1
Predicted and actual output were the same.

3.4    "/" always gives a double, even with two ints.
3      "~/" is integer division, so 17 ~/ 5 = 3.
2      17 % 5 leaves remainder 2.
3      In Dart % is never negative. -17 % 5 = 3.
-3     ~/ cuts toward zero. -3.4 becomes -3.
-17    The method .abs() runs first, then the minus. So it is -(17.abs()).
false  0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 because doubles are not exact.
true   == compares the value, so 10 and 10.0 are equal.

A2 (x = 7, y = 47)
z = 7*2 + 47~/4 = 14 + 11 = 25, then z += 7 gives 32.
Predicted and actual output were the same.

32     the value of z.
true   32 is even.
327    '$z' is "32" and '$x' is "7", and + joins them as text.

Type of z: int.
Dart looks at the right side (x*2 + y~/4). int * int is int and
int ~/ int is int, so z is an int and cannot change type later.

